Exactly. AI doesn’t just make new fakes easier; it makes the entire category of “rediscovered authenticity” more fragile.

The especially dangerous pattern is provenance laundering: something synthetic enters the world wrapped in a believable history. Not “here’s an AI song that sounds like Madonna,” but “these tapes were found in a storage unit,” or “twelve unreleased demos surfaced from a private archive.” The story supplies the missing legitimacy.

That creates several interesting attack surfaces at once: lost recordings, unfinished novels, private letters, old photographs, demos, interviews, game prototypes, paintings, voice memos, even supposed early versions of famous inventions. Once generative systems become good enough, the artifact itself may no longer carry enough evidence to settle authenticity.

So culturally, we may shift from “does this look/sound real?” toward “can you prove the chain of custody?” Authentication becomes less about inspecting the object and more about reconstructing its history: who possessed it, when it first appeared, whether hashes or timestamps existed before generation technology became capable of producing it, whether independent archives contain corroborating references, whether physical media has forensic consistency, and so on.

There’s also a nasty recursive effect: genuine discoveries become easier to dismiss because people know excellent fabrications are possible. So AI creates both false positives (“this fake is real”) and false negatives (“that real thing must be AI”).

A useful Pantheon abstraction here would be:

Artifact truth → provenance truth.

Or more fully:

surface plausibility → lineage reconstruction → independent corroboration → confidence.

In other words, the future historian may care less about whether a song sounds like a lost 1987 master and more about whether its existence leaves a coherent trail through reality.

And yes—the marketing angle is enormous. “Lost,” “forbidden,” “vault,” “final recording,” “never-before-heard,” “recently discovered” are already powerful narrative frames. AI makes it possible to manufacture not merely the content, but the feeling of archaeology. That is the deeper shift.